# Trust store for `git log --show-signature`, `%G?` and `git verify-commit`.
# SSH has no keyring, so git cannot infer trust from user.signingkey -- without
# this file every verification command errors out instead of reporting a result.
#
# Format: <principal> [options] <keytype> <base64-key>
# The principal is the identity the key is trusted as, and is what git reports
# as %GS. It is matched by key, not by committer email, so it is set to the
# commit email to keep the reported signer consistent with authored commits.
# namespaces="git" limits the trust to git signatures, so the key is not
# implicitly trusted for other ssh-keygen -Y signing namespaces.

